# Planning

The fundamental premise here is that when executing the design and implementation using eh traces, we are once again understanding it through the design. The inputs in the traces are impulses into the design and the behavior is the response. The order of those inputs is preserved by the trace and is reintroduced into the design.

# Executing Designs from Traces

My current thoughts on how I want to proceed are as follows:
- Parse the execution trace to extract all the inputs using the logged arguments.
- Save these inputs in an array.
- When the design runtime is executed, instead of getting inputs from the user, pull them from the saved inputs.

Initially, this will just prove out how preserved environments can be used to test the behavior of the design. However, this will become more useful when an invariant is learnt from a failed root cause analysis, then the trace contains an environment that allows the design to prove it prevents the semantically invalid state from persisting in its reality.

## Executing Implementation from Traces

The inputs are happening in a given order, when we parse the log file to collect all the arguments, we are collecting those inputs in that order. The behavior of the design itself is what changes in response to the environment, the inputs are from the trace file and they are fixed conditions under which the design is being evaluated. 

In fact, this extends very cleanly to distributed systems, each impulse into a distributed system creates a response in the design, the actual temporal order of the impulses can be preserved because it is a controlled test environment. You can test what were traditionally very complex distributed configurations that were hard to reproduce in a very clean way.

Using this, the implementation can be tested using the preserved environments in a very effective way and it isn't very different from testing the design itself in this way.